-- CK-141: a dependency's type error does not become the compiler's fault in
-- the module that compares the poisoned value. `A.f` is `<error>` in `A`'s
-- interface; `T` compares it with `==`. At 8b98464 the Debug binary panics
-- "I7: 1 instruction(s) whose evidence tree does not add up" in `T`
-- (ReleaseFast: two INTERNAL ERRORs at `T.beni:5:9`): the resolver rejects
-- a wanted on an `err` receiver in silence, and P6 and the I7 assert read
-- "no error in THIS module" as "no poison reached it". Also `A.f < 1`,
-- `[ A.f ] == [ 1 ]`, `List.sort [ A.f ]`, `A.f.eq 1`, and a NAMING ERROR
-- in `A` instead of a type error. Expected: exactly `A`'s one error, and
-- nothing in `T`.
pub f =
    1 + "x"
